## Insulin

You will usually take insulin in one of two ways:

- **injections**, using an insulin pen. Most people take a long-acting insulin once or twice a day, plus fast-acting insulin with meals
- **an insulin pump**, a small device that gives insulin through a thin tube under your skin all day

Your team will help you work out your doses. Learning to **count carbohydrates** helps you match insulin to what you eat.

## Checking your glucose

NICE says everyone with type 1 diabetes should be offered **continuous glucose monitoring (CGM)**. A small sensor on your arm or tummy measures your glucose all the time and sends it to your phone or a reader. This means far fewer finger-prick tests.

## Hybrid closed loop systems

A **hybrid closed loop** system links a sensor to a pump and adjusts your insulin automatically. NICE has recommended them for many people with type 1 diabetes, and the NHS is rolling them out over several years. Ask your diabetes team whether you can get one.

## Education courses

Courses such as **DAFNE** teach you how to adjust insulin, count carbohydrates and manage exercise, illness and alcohol. NICE says everyone with type 1 diabetes should be offered a course. Ask your team.

## Hypos (low blood sugar)

A **hypo** is when your glucose drops **below 4 mmol/L**. Signs include feeling shaky, sweaty, hungry, confused or irritable.

1. Have **15 to 20g of fast-acting sugar**, such as a glass of non-diet fizzy drink or fruit juice, or glucose tablets.
2. **Check again after 10 to 15 minutes**. If you are still below 4, repeat step 1.
3. When you are above 4, have a snack or meal if one is due.

{% callout type="danger" title="Severe hypo" %}
If someone with diabetes is **unconscious or having a seizure**, do not give them anything by mouth. Put them in the recovery position and **call 999**. If they have a glucagon kit and you are trained, you can use it.
{% /callout %}

## Being ill

Illness can raise your glucose, even if you are not eating. Follow your **sick day rules**: never stop your insulin, check your glucose and ketones more often, and drink plenty of fluids. Contact your team or NHS 111 if ketones are high.

